Approach: Palindrome Linked List
The idea is to check whether the linked list reads the same forward and backward.

We can do this in O(n) time and O(1) extra space using three steps:

Find the middle of the linked list.
Reverse the second half.
Compare the first half with the reversed second half.
1. Find the middle using Slow & Fast pointers
ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head;

slow moves one step
fast moves two steps
while (fast && fast->next)
    slow = slow->next, fast = fast->next->next;

When the loop ends, slow is at the middle of the list.

For:

1 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1

slow reaches:

1 → 2 → 3 ← slow

2. Reverse the second half
Your code starts reversing from the node after slow:

prev = slow;
slow = slow->next;
prev->next = NULL;

Then:

while (slow)
    temp = slow->next,
    slow->next = prev,
    prev = slow,
    slow = temp;

The important idea is:

Before:

1 → 2 → 3    2 → 1
             ↑
          second half

After reversing:

1 → 2 → 3 ← 2 ← 1

prev becomes the head of the reversed portion.

3. Compare both halves
Now:

fast = head;
slow = prev;

So:

fast starts from the beginning.
slow starts from the end of the original list, because the second half has been reversed.
Then compare:

while (slow)
    if (fast->val != slow->val)
        return false;
    else
        fast = fast->next,
        slow = slow->next;

If any pair is different:

return false;

Otherwise, all corresponding values match:

return true;

Example
For:

1 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1

After finding the middle:

First half:   1 → 2 → 3
Second half:  2 → 1

Reverse second half:

First half:   1 → 2 → 3
Reversed:           1 ← 2

Comparison:

1 == 1 ✓
2 == 2 ✓

All match → Palindrome.

For:

1 → 2 → 3 → 4

the comparison will eventually find:

1 != 4

→ false.

Why prev = slow?
This part can initially look confusing:

prev = slow;
slow = slow->next;
prev->next = NULL;

Here slow is the middle node, and the code includes the middle node in the reversed portion.

For an odd-length list:

1 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1
          ↑
        middle

It temporarily separates:

1 → 2 → 3

2 → 1

and then reverses the second part while using 3 as the starting prev.

The comparison only continues while slow is non-null, so the middle node doesn't cause a problem.

Complexity
Finding middle → O(n)
Reversing second half → O(n)
Comparing → O(n)
Therefore:

Time Complexity:  O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Code-
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPalindrome(ListNode* head) {
        ListNode *slow = head, *fast = head, *prev, *temp;
        while (fast && fast->next)
            slow = slow->next, fast = fast->next->next;
        prev = slow, slow = slow->next, prev->next = NULL;
        while (slow)
            temp = slow->next, slow->next = prev, prev = slow, slow = temp;
        fast = head, slow = prev;
        while (slow)
            if (fast->val != slow->val) return false;
            else fast = fast->next, slow = slow->next;
        return true;
    }
};
